The builder's house-style rules (src/artifactsmith/builder.py, SYSTEM prompt, rule 7) include material
adapted from third-party works:

1. frontend-design skill, from https://github.com/anthropics/skills (skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md)
   Copyright Anthropic, PBC. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
   A copy of the license is in licenses/Apache-2.0-frontend-design.txt.
   Changes: kept only the restraint, anti-default, typography, quality-floor and copy guidance, reworded
   as short prompt rules; dropped the "distinctive studio / aesthetic risk" direction.

2. Humanizer skill, from https://github.com/blader/humanizer
   Copyright (c) 2025 Siqi Chen. Licensed under the MIT License (copy in licenses/MIT-humanizer.txt). Changes: condensed a subset of its writing-pattern rules into prompt rules.

Renderers (see docs/formats.md) use WeasyPrint (BSD-3-Clause), python-docx (MIT), openpyxl (MIT), and CairoSVG (GNU LGPLv3) to rasterize chart and diagram SVG for Word.

Markdown link sanitization uses:

3. markdown-it-py, from https://github.com/executablebooks/markdown-it-py
   Copyright (c) 2020 ExecutableBookProject. Licensed under the MIT License
   (copy in licenses/MIT-markdown-it-py.txt). Also includes the original markdown-it
   JavaScript license text in that project's distribution.

4. linkify-it-py, from https://github.com/tsutsu3/linkify-it-py
   Copyright (c) 2020 tsutsu3. Licensed under the MIT License
   (copy in licenses/MIT-linkify-it-py.txt).

Fast HTML builds parse Markdown containers, attributes, definition lists, and task lists with:

5. mdit-py-plugins, from https://github.com/executablebooks/mdit-py-plugins
   Copyright (c) 2015 Vitaly Puzrin, Alex Kocharin (markdown-it container plugin).
   Licensed under the MIT License (copy in licenses/MIT-mdit-py-plugins.txt).

Fast-path chart axes (src/artifactsmith/layout/chart_scale.py) adapt one rule from:

6. answer-me-with-html, from https://github.com/QingYunA/answer-me-with-html
   Copyright (c) QingYunA. Licensed under the MIT License.
   The line axis includes 0 when the smallest value is at most a third of the largest,
   because a line encodes position rather than length (src/components/chart-scale.js).
   The Python is not a copy of that file. Tick steps of 1, 2, 2.5, and 5 are the usual nice-number scale.

Fast-path diagrams (src/artifactsmith/layout/diagram_parse.py, diagram_seq.py) adapt the
line grammar, and the idea that a sequence column grows with its label, from the same project:

7. answer-me-with-html, from https://github.com/QingYunA/answer-me-with-html
   Copyright (c) QingYunA. Licensed under the MIT License.
   Flow, sequence, timeline, and tree fences follow that project's line grammar
   (src/components/flow.js, sequence.js, timeline.js, tree.js): `A -> B: label`,
   a dashed `-->`, `note A: text`, `date | title`, and indented trees.
   Sequence columns widen so the label is not squeezed into the shaft (sequence.js).
   The Python is not a copy of those files. Drawings are static inline SVG.
